media: h713-hdmirx: the presets are the firmware's own mode table

The device does not lock on what this driver believes about timings, it locks
on a table of its own. The display firmware's mode list is the TFD module
"THDMI_ModeDetect" in database.TSE, 152 records of 54 bytes, which U-Boot
loads into the TSE window of the MIPS reservation (1 MiB at ARM 0x4be41000)
and leaves there for the boot; HY310 and HY300 Pro carry the same 152 records
byte for byte (T1, 21.09.2026). Its match rule is all-or-nothing and stops at
the first record that fits (GetMatchLevel MIPS 0x8b133290, GetSignalIndex
0x8b1337cc), and a mode the table does not carry never gets a capture window
at all: 1600x900 on 21.09.2026 left INCAP at the previous geometry, the
firmware logged "Match signal failed!" and the wall showed the console.

So where the device tree names the window (allwinner,firmware-tse, added to
sun50i-h713.dtsi in this patch, optional) that table becomes the driver's
list. At probe the window is memremap()ed write-back, read and unmapped
again; the array is found the way the serialiser leaves it findable - the
module name once, the end of the record array 22 bytes before it, then record
steps back while the leading dword stays a vendor signal id 0x0002xxxx - and
the enabled records are copied into an array of this driver's, in table order.
Nothing keeps a pointer into a region no driver owns. The table is refused,
with the built-in presets left in charge, when the property is absent, when
the window holds no module name or two of them, when the count is 0 or 255 or
more, or when no enabled record reads 1920x1080 active / 2200x1125 total /
60 Hz - the raster every source here sends. One line at probe says which:
"mode table: 152 records from the firmware's database.TSE (129 enabled)" or
"mode table: built-in presets (<reason>)".

h713_hdmirx_match_preset() then matches the measured INCAP geometry and the
measured rate against that table with the firmware's own rule: the active
size within +-5 pixels and +-3 lines where the record says so (+52), the
totals otherwise with the measured line count halved when interlaced, the
frame rate within 1.0 Hz at or above 30 Hz and 0.5 Hz below, the interlace
flag equal, and the first record that passes wins. The three checks the ARM
cannot make - sync width, sync polarity, back porch - count as passed; the
firmware skips the sync-width one in all 152 records itself, and the other
two are on in nine and twelve records. A hit reports the record's nominal
timing: its totals, its rate, its scan type, pixelclock = HTotal * VTotal *
rate, and CAN_REDUCE_FPS | REDUCED_FPS on a 1000/1001 row. Ten enabled
records (the frame-packing rasters) carry no active size at all and match on
their totals; there the measured active size is kept, which is what the
firmware does with its own capture window.

The rates are IEEE-754 doubles in the records, so they are decoded to
thousandths of a hertz by hand - the kernel has no floating point. All 152
decode to within a thousandth of the double (model, work/T2).

Without the property, and with a refused table, everything is as before: the
kernel's preset list, the same tolerances, the same result. The ring geometry
(0136f) is untouched - it reads INCAP, not this table.

Host model over all 152 rows and over the sixteen modes measured on
21.09.2026 in umbau/work/T2/model, against T1's re-implementation of the
firmware's rule.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

